<node id="延迟分位数">
  <data key="d0">延迟分位数</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">延迟分位数（Latency Percentiles）is a statistical performance metric used to analyze the distribution of response times within computational systems. By quantifying latency at specific percentile thresholds—such as P50, P95, and P99—it differentiates between typical fast responses, average cases, and rare outliers or worst-case scenarios, thereby providing a detailed view of response time variability and system health. This metric is widely adopted as a core monitoring indicator across diverse environments, including query-processing platforms, streaming computation systems, multichannel recall frameworks, embedding models, vector databases, query rewriting processes, semantic caching systems, knowledge graph systems, data masking operations, and tokenization or word segmentation algorithms. It is consistently recognized as one of the three primary performance indicators within evaluation benchmark systems—particularly in contexts like semantic caching and embedding model systems—where it measures latency distribution across percentiles to assess system reliability, efficiency, and response time consistency.

The percentile-based approach of 延迟分位数 serves as an essential benchmark for system optimization, troubleshooting, and reliability enhancement. It is used to identify slower operations, track latency distribution across quantiles, and assess overall system responsiveness and consistency. By focusing on latency distribution rather than averages alone, it helps distinguish between typical performance and edge cases, ensuring that both normal and extreme latencies are monitored for comprehensive evaluation. As a core monitoring metric, it provides a statistical measure (e.g., P99) for message processing latency, and is particularly relevant in熔断降级 systems and消息队列 (message queue) environments, where it measures the latency distribution of message processing. Overall, 延迟分位数 is a key metric for monitoring system latency distribution, supporting detailed insights into system behavior, troubleshooting, and continuous improvement in modern computational systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A core monitoring metric in streaming computing systems.</data>
